  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather will prevail this week \ncontinuing into the weekend with no significant weather hazards \nanticipated. \n\nAloft, a closed low will traverse portions of Ontario and the \nHudson Bay and into Quebec through Friday, with long wave \ntroughing across much of the northeastern US. Mid-level ridging \nwill briefly build into the region for Saturday. At the surface,\nhigh pressure currently centered just offshore the Mid-Atlantic\nand New England coast will shift slowly eastward through \ntonight. A weak cold front will pass through the region during \nthe morning and afternoon hours Thursday.\n\nAfter the cooler temperatures the past couple of days, a brief \nwarmup is anticipated, with lows tonight generally in the low to\nmid 50s and highs on Thursday in the mid 70s. The cold frontal \npassage on Thursday will usher in drier air and nudge \ntemperatures down a bit for Thursday night and into the first \npart of the weekend. Near- normal temperatures are expected \nThursday night through Saturday, with lows in the mid 40s to low\n50s and highs in the low to mid 70s for most locations. The \ncold frontal passage itself is expected to be dry, so tranquil \nconditions are expected through Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The remnants of what is now Tropical Storm \nIsaias will approach the area later this weekend bringing \nchances for rain as early as Sunday.\n\nThe primary forecast concern for the latter part of this \nweekend and perhaps into early next week will be the remnants of\nwhat is currently Tropical Storm Isaias in the Gulf. It is \nexpected to intensify into a hurricane by Thursday morning, and \nmake landfall somewhere along the northern Gulf coast Friday \nnight as it begins interacting with an upper trough. From there,\nit is expected to undergo extratropical transition and continue\nnorthward into portions of the Ohio Valley through Saturday \nnight, and then northeastward in the direction of our area on \nSunday. \n\nTiming discrepancies remain amongst model guidance this \nafternoon, but there is strong agreement that the remnants of \nIsaias will bring some impacts to the region Sunday and perhaps \ninto early Monday. At this point in time, it appears that \nwidespread rain will be the primary impact. It should be noted \nthat PWATs are modeled to be in the 1.5-2 inch range, so some \nperiods of heavy rainfall will be possible. WPC has placed most \nof the region in a Marginal Risk (1 of 4) Excessive Rainfall \nOutlook for Sunday.\n\nIt is too early to specify exact rainfall amounts, but \nconfidence is higher than normal that a large portion of the \narea could end up with 1 to 2 inches of rain, with higher \namounts certainly not out of the question. We will continue to \nmonitor trends over the coming days.",
